\f1\fs22 << DUNHILTON & TEAM are watching a vid of this \'85 >>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Doors sweep back and the view lurches unsteadily through a room where Time had once thrown a party, then left without cleaning up. Shy light, as if embarrassed by the room's decline, comes down in blurred puddles. Tarnished chandeliers resolve briefly into focus, their cut-glass dulled by years of disregard. Patches of wallpaper, once as vibrant as a rose garden at high noon, peel and droop.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What is this place?\'92 the Director of Moral Observance asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s the new Parliament House of the Elvi,\'92 Gerry\'97acting Commissioner of Elvi Rights\'97answered. \'91It was a brothel called 
\f2\i Clambake
\f1\i0  till they repurposed it.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Moral Observance pursed his lips and chewed on the information. He had no problem with either a brothel or a parliament, but wondered about the propriety of one becoming the other. If only he knew history better.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91They\'92re forming a government.\'92 Dunhilton swore like a wharfie, betraying his blue-collar ancestry.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The holo view pans past heavy curtains, the colour of a cabaret singer's lipstick, hang faded and moth-eaten against the wall, leading the eye to a grand staircase.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton could almost smell the moldy squalor of the dank space.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A crowd occupying most of the ground floor; a crowd of leather, cowboy outfits, army uniforms, once glamorous evening dresses, and sequinned jumpsuits shimmering like disco balls caught in a cyclone. In the eye of the crowded storm is a figure in a suit studded with twinkling rhinestones.
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91My fellow Elvi,\'92 the High Elvis says on the vid. \'91Praise the Dawkins.\'92
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Who the tax is the Dawkins?\'92 Dunhilton demanded as the High Elvis on his screen thanked everyone for their support in appointing him to his office.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We\'92re still looking into that,\'92 Gerry answered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And what is is he standing in?\'92 Dunhilton went on. Everyone else at the table peered deep into the holo image, squinting hard, hoping to be the first to answer the question.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s a spa bath,\'92 said the Director of Moral Observance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton laughed with equal measure of disbelief and fury.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis continues with a speech.
\f1\i0  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What the hell is he talking about?\'92 Moral observance raised his eyebrows so high, they almost smoothed away the bags under his eyes.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I have a burning love for all of you in the Ghetto.\'92
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The cabinet listened in silence, except for Moral Observance who injected comments like, \'91It\'92s just a pile of slogans,\'92 and \'91It\'92s gibberish, pure gibberish.\'92\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An eruption of cheering overwhelms the recording device\'92s microphone.
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91It\'92s time we reclaimed our sense of pride, and our self-determination.\'92\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton drummed fingers on the table. Gerry gestured, 
\f2\i keep calm.
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Our spirits will rise from the shadows of the Ghetto  \'85\'92
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91He\'92s inciting the Elvi to rise up,\'92 Dunhilton accused.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91\'85 and even in the Pale too \'85\'92
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Now all the faces in the conference room turned sombre as the cabinet considered the real possibility the Elvi might mobilise outside the Ghetto. Gerry\'92s eyes turned as steely as Dunhilton\'92s. Moral Observance\'92s eyes glistened from too much blood sugar. Defence stared at the holo-vid, recognising both the threat and the opportunity to deploy some of his favourite toys.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The camera pulls back and pans as the crowd chants, \'91High Elvis! High Elvis! \'85 Praise the Dawkins! \'85 Bless the Holy Momma!\'92\

\f1\i0 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A grim Dunhilton tightened his lips and stared at Gerry with the intensity of one of Defence\'92s flamethrowers. \'91The Elvi have been worshipping Sarah Jacobson, apparently for nothing more virtuous than being knocked up and single.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Indeed your Serene Corporateness,\'92 Gerry answered with the confidence of a salesperson who had a product worth buying. \'91The previous Commissioner of Elvi Rights completely missed the significance of these developments.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton froze his eyes mid roll. 
\f2\i There\'92s no need to keep selling it, Gerry
\f1\i0 , he thought. 
\f2\i I\'92ve already ordered her retirement
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91What if her baby is a half Airborne, half Elvi mutant?\'92 Moral Observance blanched at the heresy of it, his face whiter than a KKK hood.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Defence remained quiet and calculating.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We will take our music to the rest of Ulyssia and on to the rest of the world!\'92 the High Elvis promises.
\f1\i0 \

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91We\'92ll be famous! We\'92ll be richer than the Airborne!\'92 the crowd answers.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Holy taxing taxing taxing taxing \'85\'92 Swearing had ceased being a comfort to Dunhilton, so he grimaced and just said, \'91Fuck,\'92 and waved a disdained hand through the vid to shut it down.
